<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.9.2: Finding Products of Differences</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01886</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.9.2: Finding Products of Differences</md:title>
    <md:uuid>33e95e8a-9ff2-434b-9146-ebbd2d849ec8</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Show that \((x-1)(x-1)\) and \(x^2-2x+1\) are equivalent expressions by drawing a diagram or applying the
        <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">distributive
          property</term>. Show your reasoning.
      </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para class="os-raise-text-bold">Drawing a Diagram:</para>
  
  <table class="os-raise-doubleheadertable">
    <thead>
      <row>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis"/>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">\(x\)</entry>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">\(-1\)</entry>
      </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <row>
        <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">\(x\)</entry>
        <entry>\(x^2\)</entry>
        <entry>\(-x\)</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">\(-1\)</entry>
        <entry>\(-x\)</entry>
        <entry>\(1\)</entry>
      </row>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  
  <para>The sum of the partial products are \(x^2+-x+-x+1\) or \(x^2-2x+1\).</para>
  <para class="os-raise-text-bold">Applying the Distributive Property:</para>
  <para>Applying the distributive property to \((x-1)(x-1)\) gives \(x^2+x \cdot (-1)+(-1) \cdot x+(-1)^2=
    x^2-x-x+1=x^2-2x+1\).</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>For each expression, write an equivalent expression. Show your reasoning.</item>
</list>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> \((x+1)(x-1)\) </para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(x^2-1\). For example, applying the distributive property to \((x+1)(x-1)\) gives \(x^2+x-x-1\), which equals
    \(x^2-1\).</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> \((x-2)(x+3)\) </para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para> \(x^2+x-6\), because \((x+3)(x-2)=x^2-2x+3x-6=x^2+x-6\).</para>
  <para>Or using a diagram:</para>
  
  <table class="os-raise-doubleheadertable">
    <thead>
      <row>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis"/>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">\(x\)</entry>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">\(3\)</entry>
      </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <row>
        <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">\(x\)</entry>
        <entry>\(x^2\)</entry>
        <entry>\(3x\)</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">\(-2\)</entry>
        <entry>\(-2x\)</entry>
        <entry>\(-6\)</entry>
      </row>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  
  <para>Adding the partial products in the four sub-rectangles gives \(x^2+3x+-2x+-6\), which equals \(x^2+x-6\).</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> \((x-2)^2\) </para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(x^2-4x+4\), because \((x-2)(x-2)=x^2-2x-2x+4=x^2-4x+4\).</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><title>Video: Finding Products of Differences</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about finding products of differences.</para>
